UNIT isBuffer
KIND FUNCTIONAL
ORACLE-CLASS deterministic
SIG isBuffer(obj: any) -> any        # declared :boolean in index.d.ts, but the IMPLEMENTATION does NOT coerce — see node-4
DEPENDS-ON none

# ============================================================================
# OVERVIEW
# ============================================================================
# Determine whether `obj` is a Node.js Buffer WITHOUT requiring the `buffer`
# module. Detection is purely duck-typed: it asks the object's own constructor
# whether it considers `obj` a Buffer (Buffer exposes a static `Buffer.isBuffer`).
# Pure, total over codec-faithful inputs (no ambient seam — no clock/RNG/io/net).
# Single expression; the entire body is one short-circuiting `&&` chain.
#
# EXACT SOURCE (index.js, the whole module body):
#   module.exports = function isBuffer (obj) {
#     return obj != null && obj.constructor != null &&
#       typeof obj.constructor.isBuffer === 'function' && obj.constructor.isBuffer(obj)
#   }

# ============================================================================
# BEHAVIOR  — ordered short-circuit pipeline (JS `&&`, left to right)
# ============================================================================
# Evaluate the four operands of `&&` in order. `&&` returns the FIRST falsy
# operand (as-is) OR, if all are truthy, the LAST operand (as-is). There is NO
# Boolean() wrapping anywhere. This makes the return type and exact value
# load-bearing — see node-4.
#
# node-1  GUARD: `obj != null`
#   - LOOSE inequality against null. This is true UNLESS obj is `null` OR
#     `undefined` (the only two values loosely-equal to null).
#   - Note: 0, "", NaN, false are all `!= null` === true; they pass this guard.
#   - If FALSE -> the whole `&&` short-circuits and returns the boolean `false`.
#     (When obj is null:      `null != null`      -> false -> returns false.
#      When obj is undefined: `undefined != null` -> false -> returns false.)
#
# node-2  GUARD: `obj.constructor != null`
#   - Reads the `constructor` property of obj (own or inherited via prototype
#     chain). For primitives (number/string/boolean/bigint/symbol) that pass
#     node-1, the value is auto-boxed for the property read, yielding the
#     wrapper constructor (Number/String/Boolean/BigInt/Symbol) — non-null,
#     so node-2 passes for primitives.
#   - LOOSE inequality: passes unless `obj.constructor` is `null` or `undefined`.
#     * An object created via `Object.create(null)` has no `constructor` ->
#       reads `undefined` -> node-2 FALSE -> returns boolean `false`.
#     * `{ constructor: null }` -> node-2 FALSE -> returns boolean `false`.
#   - If FALSE -> short-circuits, returns boolean `false`.
#   - (No try/catch: if reading `.constructor` were to throw via a custom
#     getter that throws, the throw would propagate — but `.constructor` is a
#     plain data property in all codec-faithful inputs, so this is N/A here.)
#
# node-3  GUARD: `typeof obj.constructor.isBuffer === 'function'`
#   - Reads `obj.constructor.isBuffer` and checks its typeof is exactly the
#     string 'function'.
#   - FALSE (returns boolean `false`) when isBuffer is absent (undefined), or
#     present but not a function: e.g. `isBuffer: true`, `isBuffer: 1`,
#     `isBuffer: "x"`, `isBuffer: null`, `isBuffer: {}` all yield typeof !=
#     'function' -> returns boolean `false`.
#   - A real Buffer instance: `buf.constructor` is `Buffer`, and
#     `Buffer.isBuffer` is a function -> node-3 passes.
#   - A Uint8Array: `u8.constructor` is `Uint8Array`, which has NO static
#     `isBuffer` -> `undefined` -> typeof 'undefined' -> node-3 FALSE ->
#     returns boolean `false`. (This is the canonical Uint8Array=false case.)
#   - If FALSE -> short-circuits, returns boolean `false`.
#
# node-4  CALL (final operand): `obj.constructor.isBuffer(obj)`
#   - Invoke the static `isBuffer` with `obj.constructor` as `this` and `obj`
#     as the sole argument.
#   - CRITICAL — NO COERCION: the value returned by isBuffer is returned by the
#     unit AS-IS. The unit does NOT do `Boolean(...)` / `!!...`. Therefore:
#       * real Buffer:                       Buffer.isBuffer(buf) -> true (boolean)
#       * spoof `isBuffer: () => true`:      -> true   (boolean)
#       * spoof `isBuffer: () => false`:     -> false  (boolean)
#       * spoof `isBuffer: () => 1`:         -> 1      (number)   <-- not `true`
#       * spoof `isBuffer: () => 0`:         -> 0      (number)   <-- not `false`
#       * spoof `isBuffer: () => "yes"`:     -> "yes"  (string)
#       * spoof `isBuffer: () => 42`:        -> 42     (number)
#       * spoof `isBuffer: () => null`:      -> null
#       * spoof `isBuffer: () => undefined`: -> undefined
#       * spoof `isBuffer: () => []`:        -> []     (array)
#     The index.d.ts type annotation (`: boolean`) is OPTIMISTIC; the runtime
#     value is exactly whatever the callee returns. A blind re-emitter MUST
#     return this value uncoerced.
#   - THROW PASSTHROUGH: if the callee throws, isBuffer throws the same error
#     (no try/catch). (Only reachable via a live throwing function -> NOT
#     codec-faithful -> see SCOPE out-of-envelope; not emitted by genInputs.)
#
# RESULT TYPE SUMMARY
#   - If any of node-1..node-3 is the short-circuit point: return boolean `false`.
#   - If all guards pass: return the raw result of node-4 (any JS value).

# ============================================================================
# WORKED IN-SCOPE CASES (self-consistent with genInputs; all codec-faithful)
# ============================================================================
#   real Node Buffer (e.g. Buffer.from([1,2,3]))      -> true   (genuine TRUE branch, node-4)
#   empty Buffer (Buffer.alloc(0))                     -> true
#   new Uint8Array([...])                              -> false  (node-3: Uint8Array has no static isBuffer)
#   {} (plain object)                                  -> false  (node-3: Object has no static isBuffer)
#   "abc" / "" (string)                                -> false  (node-3: String has no static isBuffer)
#   [1,2,3] / [] (array)                               -> false  (node-3: Array has no static isBuffer)
#   null                                               -> false  (node-1)
#   undefined                                          -> false  (node-1)
#   42 / 0 (number)                                    -> false  (node-3: Number has no static isBuffer)
#   true / false (boolean)                             -> false  (node-3: Boolean has no static isBuffer)
#   5n (bigint)                                        -> false  (node-3: BigInt has no static isBuffer)
#   /x/ (RegExp)                                       -> false  (node-3)
#   new Set([1]) / new Map()                           -> false  (node-3)
#   new ArrayBuffer(8) / new DataView(...) / Float64Array -> false (node-3)
#   Object.create(null)                                -> false  (node-2: no constructor)
#   { constructor: null }                              -> false  (node-2)
#   { constructor: {} }                                -> false  (node-3: no isBuffer)
#   { constructor: { isBuffer: true } }                -> false  (node-3: isBuffer not a function)
#   { constructor: { isBuffer: 1 } }                   -> false  (node-3: isBuffer not a function)
#   { constructor: { isBuffer: "x" } }                 -> false  (node-3: isBuffer not a function)
#   { constructor: { isBuffer: null } }                -> false  (node-3)

# ============================================================================
# ORACLE
# ============================================================================
ORACLE value -> oracle.json
# Pure value oracle: expected = isBuffer(args[0]). For codec-faithful inputs the
# expected is the raw (possibly non-boolean) return value, captured by the harness
# from the real package. Throw-paths are not reachable by codec-faithful inputs.

# ============================================================================
# SCOPE
# ============================================================================
# IN-CONTRACT LOGIC (reproduce exactly):
#   The entire 4-node short-circuit `&&` chain above, INCLUDING the no-coercion
#   passthrough of node-4's return value and the boolean `false` produced by
#   short-circuiting at node-1/2/3.
#
# CARRIED DATA: none. (No literal tables; the unit is a 2-line expression.)
#
# OUT-OF-CONTRACT / OUT-OF-ENVELOPE (documented for the blind re-emitter, but
# NOT emitted by genInputs because the values are not codec-round-trippable):
#   - Adversarial TRUE via a LIVE function: `{ constructor: { isBuffer: () => true } }`.
#     A live function does not survive the oracle codec (JSON drops it, leaving
#     `{ constructor: {} }` which then returns false), so it would poison the
#     held-out. The re-emitter still reconstructs node-4 from this spec: when
#     `constructor.isBuffer` IS a function, the unit calls it and returns its
#     value uncoerced. The genuine TRUE branch is exercised instead by a REAL
#     Node Buffer (codec-faithful), which passes node-4 identically.
#   - Non-boolean returns from a live spoof (`() => 1`, `() => "yes"`, etc.):
#     same non-round-trippability; documented in node-4 so the re-emitter's
#     uncoerced-return behavior is fully specified.
#   - THROW passthrough (`() => { throw }`): live function, not codec-faithful;
#     documented in node-4.
#   - A getter on `.constructor`/`.isBuffer`: not expressible via codec; data
#     properties only in-envelope.
#   - Symbol arguments: typeof 'symbol' is not codec-round-trippable as a value
#     here; behavior (-> false at node-3, BigInt/Symbol wrapper has no isBuffer)
#     is documented but not emitted.

# ============================================================================
# PROVENANCE
# ============================================================================
PROVENANCE is-buffer@2.0.5, github.com/feross/is-buffer, MIT (Feross Aboukhadijeh), source file: index.js
